{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>When you beget children and children's children, etc.</b> Since the sentence of evil is not executed swiftly, therefore the heart of the sons of men is fully set to do evil (Ecclesiastes 8:11), and a person does not feel that the Holy One, blessed be He, is prolonging His anger — yet before Him it comes at the end of days. Therefore it says, ״כִּי תוֹלִיד בָּנִים וְגוֹ׳ וְהִשְׁחַתֶּם וַעֲשִׂיתֶם פֶּסֶל״ — meaning, during this very span, in those very days when you corrupt yourselves, you will beget children and children's children according to the way of the world, and you will grow old in the land. And through this you will come to reason mistakenly: if HaShem desired to destroy us, He would by now not have remained silent — and behold, we are begetting children according to the way of the world and dwelling on the land for many days; if so, surely we bear no guilt. To this He said: ״הַעִידֹתִי בָכֶם הַיּוֹם אֶת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֶת הָאָרֶץ כִּי אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן מַהֵר מֵעַל הָאָרֶץ״ — for it is the measure of HaShem, blessed be He, not to bring troubles pressed close one upon another, as they expounded in the Midrash (Bereshit Rabbah 75:13) from the verse ״וְרֶוַח תָּשִׂימוּ בֵּין עֵדֶר וּבֵין עֵדֶר״ (Genesis 32:16) — and this is specifically when He exacts punishment immediately, without an extension of time. But when the Holy One, blessed be He, prolongs His anger for a long time, then at the end of days He brings troubles bundled and pressed close one to another, and trouble draws near to its sister. And so it was in the First Temple: they practiced idolatry for a long time from the days of Jeroboam, yet despite this He prolonged His anger toward them. And that which is written (Deuteronomy 11:17) ״וַאֲבַדְתֶּם מְהֵרָה״ — and there Rashi explained: I will not grant you an extension — and so here too it says ״כִּי אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן מַהֵר״. But surely this notion of swiftness is said concerning the troubles that will come at the end, pressed close in quick succession, one after the other." "And I have support for this interpretation. From what the Yalkut derives in parashat Eikev (§529) on the verse ״וַאֲבַדְתֶּם מְהֵרָה״ — exile after exile: they were exiled in the seventh year to Jehoiakim and in the eighth to Nebuchadnezzar; they were exiled in the eighteenth, etc. And this notion of swiftness is alluded to well in this verse: ״כִּי אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן״ — the doubling of expression indicates destruction upon destruction that will be ״מֵעַל הָאָרֶץ,״ and after the destruction ״לֹא תַאֲרִיכֻן יָמִים עָלֶיהָ כִּי הִשָּׁמֵד תִּשָּׁמֵדוּן״ — meaning, you will not lengthen days between the destruction and the annihilation, but they will be pressed close. And annihilation (hashmadah) involves greater obliteration than destruction (ibud); and so it is stated (Isaiah 8:23): ״וְהָאַחֲרוֹן הִכְבִּיד״ — 'and the latter one was heavier.' And this is a precious interpretation with which to resolve the swiftness spoken of here — for the Holy One, blessed be He, did prolong His anger toward them for a long time, except that it is said concerning the troubles pressed close: destruction after destruction, and annihilation after annihilation, and between destruction and annihilation too you will not lengthen days." "And that He called heaven and earth to witness against them — this intends to give a reason for why the Holy One, blessed be He, prolongs His anger for a long time. As the Yalkut derives in parashat Ha'azinu (§941), and this is its language: A parable of a king who handed his son over to a pedagogue, etc. So Moses said to Israel: Perhaps you think to flee from beneath the wings of the Shekhinah and to move off the earth, etc. And the substance of the matter is that for this reason the Holy One, blessed be He, prolongs His anger — because He knows there is no escaping Him, for He has handed them over to a pedagogue, that is, to heaven and earth, which cannot move at all. Therefore He said: ״הַעִידֹתִי בָכֶם אֶת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֶת הָאָרֶץ״. And further, because the hand of the witnesses is to be upon him first, as it is written (Job 20:27): ״יְגַלּוּ שָׁמַיִם עֲוֹנוֹ וְאֶרֶץ מִתְקוֹמָמָה לוֹ״. And it is possible that this is the significance of the doubling of destruction and annihilation stated here — to assign one to heaven and one to earth: ״כִּי אָבֹד״ — through heaven; and afterward ״תֹּאבֵדוּן מֵעַל הָאָרֶץ״ — through earth; and afterward ״הִשָּׁמֵד תִּשָּׁמֵדוּן״. And it is plausible to hear that this annihilation refers to what is written immediately after: ״וַעֲבַדְתֶּם שָׁם אֱלֹהִים מַעֲשֵׂה יְדֵי אָדָם עֵץ וָאֶבֶן״ — that is, actual shemad, forcible apostasy. And the reason for the matter is: for this reason the Holy One, blessed be He, brings it about that they come to the point of shemadot — in order that Israel may see that all the gods of the nations are idols and there is no benefit in them, and then they will say, 'I will go and return to my first husband' (Hosea 2:9). And this is what is stated: ״וּבִקַּשְׁתֶּם מִשָּׁם אֶת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — 'there' is not said, but rather 'from there,' meaning that this very idolatry will be the cause to bring you back to YHWH your Elohim." "And Mahariv explained the word 'from there' (מִשָּׁם): because of the anger of the oppressor, they will worship wood and stone outwardly, while their heart is constantly toward HaShem. Therefore it says: ״וּבִקַּשְׁתֶּם מִשָּׁם וְגוֹ׳ בְּכָל לְבָבְךָ״ — specifically their heart will be aligned with HaShem, for the mouth is not permitted to speak." "Another matter: therefore it says ״כִּי תוֹלִיד בָּנִים וּבְנֵי בָנִים״ — if you reckon, after you have become firmly established in the land for many days, that it is yours outright and who could take it from your hand — therefore it says at the conclusion of the parashah: ״וּלְמַעַן תַּאֲרִיךְ יָמִים עַל הָאֲדָמָה אֲשֶׁר ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ נֹתֵן לְךָ כָּל הַיָּמִים״ — for each and every day He gives it to you as a new gift, not outright, but each day on condition, if you keep His commandments. And that which they expounded (Gittin 88a-b) — according to the numerical value of ״וְנוֹשַׁנְתֶּם״ they were in the land, and HaShem hastened the evil two years before the time — HaShem had already shown this very thing to Jacob in a vision, when the sun set upon him two hours before its time, as explained above in parashat Vayetze (28:11). And it is a fitting allusion."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:01.954065464Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}